	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/08/44539</id><title>COP31 Litmus Test for International Cooperation</title><updated>2026-11-08T13:59:49-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/08/44539" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/COP31.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/COP31.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/COP31-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/COP31.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
ANTALYA, Türkiye, November 8 (IPS)  - As the world heads toward &lt;span class=&quot;gvygm6 gvyl4o&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;gvyl4o&quot; data-sdui-anchor-id=&quot;commentary-GSFTCs1BwWL99EOSAiNmZyOZ3WPQriT-x8Pd59mhJkI-9fe6d212-e1b7-4310-826f-01a8642373de::26&quot;&gt;COP31 Türkiye&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt; starting this week, I’ve been reflecting on how I think COP31 could prove to be a more revealing “Transition COP”. Let us remember that the Paris Agreement came from a series of “institutional heartbeat” COPs – NOT from annual blockbuster headlines.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/08/44539&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “COP31 Litmus Test for International Cooperation”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/07/44540</id><title>‘For the first time I have my own chair’: Gaza siblings find new freedom</title><updated>2026-11-07T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/07/44540" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/11/06-11-2026-EducAid-Gaza-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/11/06-11-2026-EducAid-Gaza-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/11/06-11-2026-EducAid-Gaza-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/11/06-11-2026-EducAid-Gaza-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Gaza, 10-year-old Anas is one of three siblings born without legs and with only two fingers on each of their hands. They struggle to move around and perform daily activities like eating and writing independently. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/07/44540&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘For the first time I have my own chair’: Gaza siblings find new freedom”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44533</id><title>Behind Taliban Dress Code Arrests, Afghan Activists See a Campaign to Silence Critics</title><updated>2026-11-06T15:59:50-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44533" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/silencecritics.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/silencecritics.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/silencecritics-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/silencecritics.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
KABUL, November 6 (IPS)  - In Afghanistan, the Taliban are detaining women, even when they are wearing a hijab. Activists suspect that the issue of attire is merely a pretext used by the Taliban to hunt down critics of the regime.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44533&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Behind Taliban Dress Code Arrests, Afghan Activists See a Campaign to Silence Critics”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44532</id><title>Why Geohazards Must Be Part of the Pacific’s Resilience Agenda</title><updated>2026-11-06T10:26:40-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44532" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Mount-Bromo_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Mount-Bromo_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Mount-Bromo_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Mount-Bromo_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
BANGKOK, Thailand, November 6 (IPS)  - For years, global discussions of disaster risk in the Pacific have been dominated by climate change. Rising seas, intensifying cyclones and coastal erosion have rightly captured international attention. This focus, while warranted, should be complemented by greater recognition of geohazards, which are just as consequential and in many cases, more immediately devastating.  Addressing this gap requires a more coordinated approach, supported by sustained investment and stronger partnerships, to foster cooperation and strengthen resilience across the Pacific.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44532&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Why Geohazards Must Be Part of the Pacific’s Resilience Agenda”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44531</id><title>Sixty Years on, UN Is Finally Interpreting Freedom of Association</title><updated>2026-11-06T09:51:51-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44531" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/The-Palais-des-Nations_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/The-Palais-des-Nations_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/The-Palais-des-Nations_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/The-Palais-des-Nations_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
GENEVA, November 6 (IPS)  -  &lt;strong&gt;A right under pressure&lt;/strong&gt; - No one disputes freedom of association. It is in the Constitutions of almost every country, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, Article 22 of the ICCPR, and in regional Articles in Europe, America, and Africa. What it has never had, at the universal level, is sincere implementation by governments.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44531&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Sixty Years on, UN Is Finally Interpreting Freedom of Association”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44530</id><title>Kenya’s Civil Society Calls on COP31 Climate Negotiators To Draw From Grassroots</title><updated>2026-11-06T06:41:32-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44530" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Tharaka-seed-custodians-pray-for-seeds-at-Kibuka-shrine-ahead-of-2026-planting-season.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Tharaka-seed-custodians-pray-for-seeds-at-Kibuka-shrine-ahead-of-2026-planting-season.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Tharaka-seed-custodians-pray-for-seeds-at-Kibuka-shrine-ahead-of-2026-planting-season-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/11/Tharaka-seed-custodians-pray-for-seeds-at-Kibuka-shrine-ahead-of-2026-planting-season.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NAIROBI, November 6 (IPS)  - As the world prepares for the 31&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; United Nations Conference on Climate Change (COP31) in Antalya, Türkiye, Kenya’s priorities for the negotiations are being shaped by lived experiences of communities who are on the frontline of the climate crisis.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44530&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Kenya’s Civil Society Calls on COP31 Climate Negotiators To Draw From Grassroots”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44538</id><title>Afghanistan faces deepening humanitarian crisis amid sweeping rights restrictions</title><updated>2026-11-06T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44538" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/13-07-2026-UNICEF-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/13-07-2026-UNICEF-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/13-07-2026-UNICEF-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/07/13-07-2026-UNICEF-Afghanistan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Afghanistan stands at a “difficult crossroad” more than five years after the Taliban returned to power, a senior UN official said on Friday. Relative stability and security are being undercut by a fragile economy, sweeping curbs on rights and acute humanitarian need.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44538&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Afghanistan faces deepening humanitarian crisis amid sweeping rights restrictions”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44537</id><title>World News in Brief: Civilians killed in DR Congo, Ukrainians wrestle with return, Guterres calls out ‘normalisation’ of force</title><updated>2026-11-06T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44537" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-02-2025-UNICEF-DR-Congo-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-02-2025-UNICEF-DR-Congo-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-02-2025-UNICEF-DR-Congo-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-02-2025-UNICEF-DR-Congo-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At least 30 civilians were killed overnight on Thursday in Beni Territory, North Kivu in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, according to local sources cited by the UN aid coordination office (OCHA).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44537&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “World News in Brief: Civilians killed in DR Congo, Ukrainians wrestle with return, Guterres calls out ‘normalisation’ of force”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44536</id><title>Sudan’s suffering is plain for all to see say aid teams, as brutal war continues</title><updated>2026-11-06T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44536" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/30-09-2026-IOM-Sudan-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/30-09-2026-IOM-Sudan-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/30-09-2026-IOM-Sudan-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/30-09-2026-IOM-Sudan-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Aid teams working to help millions of people uprooted by the ongoing war in Sudan appealed on Friday for more international assistance amid desperate funding shortages, empty warehouses and overwhelming needs. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44536&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Sudan’s suffering is plain for all to see say aid teams, as brutal war continues”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44535</id><title>‘No Escape’ for refugees on the frontlines of the climate crisis</title><updated>2026-11-06T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44535" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-IOM-Somalia-flooding-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-IOM-Somalia-flooding-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-IOM-Somalia-flooding-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/23-10-2026-IOM-Somalia-flooding-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The climate emergency is an immediate crisis for refugees, with three in four – nearly 90 million people – living in countries facing high to severe exposure to hazards such as drought, floods and extreme heat. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/06/44535&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘No Escape’ for refugees on the frontlines of the climate crisis”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0053s ch:0 -->